Within-instance analysis (restricted to non-degenerate instances where F1 varies across samples) reveals that MBR utility \cite{eikema2022sampling} does correlate positively with F1 (SJ median within-instance $\rho{=}0.524$ on SciERC, $0.994$ on CoNLL), and on these instances consensus-proximity selection closes 23\% (SciERC) to 41\% (CoNLL) of the oracle gap.
However, approximately 29\% of non-degenerate SciERC instances exhibit \textit{negative} within-instance SJ--F1 correlation, indicating that for a substantial minority, higher structural consistency is actively associated with lower quality; this heterogeneity further explains why pooled positive correlation does not translate to reliable MBR selection.
Beyond this, fine-tuned models exhibit pervasive \textit{instance degeneracy}

